# /etc/logrotate.d/rsyslog on the WALL PANEL -- installed by wall-firstboot.sh
# on every boot over the distribution's file of the same name (SR-083).
#
# WHY IT REPLACES THE STOCK FILE RATHER THAN ADDING ONE BESIDE IT. logrotate
# refuses a log named by two stanzas ("duplicate log entry") and skips it, so a
# second file listing /var/log/syslog would have disabled rotation of syslog
# altogether. The list of files and the postrotate below are the stock Ubuntu
# 24.04 ones, unchanged; only the schedule and the size cap differ.
#
# WHY. On 2026-10-18 at 00:02 the weekly rotation gzipped a week of syslog that
# had grown to tens of GB (the compressed result alone was 944 MB) and filled
# the root filesystem for about two minutes; the kiosk's cookie store stopped
# persisting and the next restart lost the Pandora sign-in. Nothing capped
# rsyslog's file. journald holds the same messages under its own explicit cap
# (wall-journald.conf), so this copy only needs to be short.
#
# THE CAP: daily, or sooner once a file passes 100 MB, seven generations kept.
# `maxsize` is only checked when logrotate RUNS, and the stock logrotate.timer
# is daily -- so wall-logrotate-hourly.conf re-schedules that same timer to run
# hourly. Worst case on disk is then about one hour of growth past 100 MB in the
# live file plus one uncompressed (delaycompress) and six compressed 100 MB
# generations -- hundreds of MB, not tens of GB.
/var/log/syslog
/var/log/mail.log
/var/log/kern.log
/var/log/auth.log
/var/log/user.log
/var/log/cron.log
{
	daily
	maxsize 100M
	rotate 7
	missingok
	notifempty
	compress
	delaycompress
	sharedscripts
	postrotate
		/usr/lib/rsyslog/rsyslog-rotate
	endscript
}
